---
title: "Breakpoints"
description: "Five named thresholds in rem, read from the nearest inline-size container."
section: Foundations
sectionOrder: 1
order: 3
---

A breakpoint tier (`--grid-cols--md: 3`) applies when the nearest inline-size container is at least as wide as a threshold in `rem`. The thresholds are the same widths as the [layout tokens](/api/foundations/tokens/) (`--layout-md` is 48rem), so `md` means "an md band fits here".

| Breakpoint | Threshold | At 16px | Layout token |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `sm` | 40rem | 640px | `--layout-sm` |
| `md` | 48rem | 768px | `--layout-md` |
| `lg` | 64rem | 1024px | `--layout-lg` |
| `xl` | 80rem | 1280px | `--layout-xl` |
| `2xl` | 96rem | 1536px | `--layout-2xl` |

`rem` resolves against the root font in every container, so siblings set in different type flip at the same width, and a web font loading late never moves a threshold. The thresholds still scale with the reader's font size setting and zoom.

## Which container answers

The reset makes `html` an inline-size container, so markup outside any other container responds to the page, however narrow its own `section` or `main` is. Inside a [layout](/api/foundations/layout/), each band child is a container, so tiers inside a band follow the band. Mark anything else (a card, a sidebar, a slot) with the `container` switch (`data-ui="container"`) and the tiers inside it follow its width.

Two rules follow from how container queries work:

- An element never queries itself. A container's own tiers read the container above it.
- A container cannot size itself from its content, so give it a definite or stretched width.

Tiers are mobile-first: `--display: none; --display--md: flex` hides below `md` and shows from `md` up. Color utilities take breakpoint tiers too, and a state still beats a breakpoint (`--bg--md: black; --bg--hover: gray` is gray on hover at any width).
